# Ignore list for the generic Next.js fixture application.
#
# This directory is a test fixture, not a pnpm workspace member. The browser and
# end-to-end suites run it as the generalization target: the product indexes it,
# instruments it, captures a session against it and then restores it. The gate
# `node scripts/check-no-residual-instrumentation.mjs --path <this directory>`
# asserts that `git status --porcelain` for this path is empty afterwards, so
# every file a dev or build run leaves behind here has to be listed below. An
# unignored generated file would fail that gate for a reason that has nothing to
# do with the cleanup guarantee it exists to prove.
#
# The repository root already ignores three of the four patterns below, yet this
# file is kept self-contained on purpose:
#   - the root entry for the ambient declaration file is scoped to another
#     workspace, so only the entry below keeps this directory clean; and
#   - the integration suites copy this fixture into a temporary directory
#     outside the checkout, where repository-root rules do not apply at all.
#
# Scope is deliberately narrow: no negations, nothing tracked is ignored
# (package.json, tsconfig.json, next.config.ts and the app and src trees all
# stay in version control), and there is no environment-file entry because no
# such file may exist in this directory.

# build and dev-server output
.next/

# ambient type declarations, rewritten on every dev and build run. The root
# ignore entry for this filename is scoped to another workspace, so this line
# is the one that keeps the working tree clean here.
next-env.d.ts

# dependencies. Nothing should ever install into this directory: the fixture is
# not a workspace member and runs on a `next` binary resolved from a workspace
# that has one. Ignored as insurance against a stray local install.
node_modules/

# incremental TypeScript build state, possible because this folder's
# tsconfig.json sets `incremental: true`
*.tsbuildinfo
